  "evidence": "TEXT_D: Replace Council Tax with a Local Income Tax and raise the stamp duty threshold; abolish tuition and top‑up fees, restore grants, cut class sizes and hire 21,000 teachers; more doctors and nurses, free personal care, faster diagnosis, free eye/dental checks, rebuild NHS dentistry, and ban smoking in enclosed public places; 10,000 more police and cut red tape; Citizen’s Pension with an earnings link and Winter Fuel Payment extension; fix rail and free off‑peak buses for pensioners/disabled; state the Iraq war was wrong and aim to withdraw UK troops; green fiscal reform with 20% renewables by 2020. TEXT_G: Repeal the European Communities Act to leave the EU with a two‑year transition; aim for near‑zero net immigration via a points system and end EU preference; immediate tax cuts including halving council tax and raising inheritance tax threshold; NHS local control and allow complementary private insurance; education vouchers/tax rebates and cancel top‑up fees; reverse defence cuts; stop wind farms and build new nuclear.",
  "reasoning": "In 2005 the British electorate’s top priorities were the NHS, schools, crime, taxation/cost of living and the Iraq war, while EU withdrawal was a minority concern. TEXT_D offers detailed, costed proposals across health, education, policing, taxes, pensions, transport and Iraq, whereas TEXT_G is dominated by leaving the EU and immigration with comparatively fewer concrete commitments on public services, so TEXT_D better matches those priorities.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_D"
}
